FILE MANAGEMENT COMMANDS :
========================================

1.list all files and directories

a.long list shows detailed information directories , owner, size

command 1 : ll 

b.short list shows only files 

command 2 : ls (one line)

c.This command same as ll

command 3 : ls -l 

d.Shows the hidden file

command : ls -la or ll -a

here : 

l = long list
a = all files including hidden files

e. Shows hidden file in short list

command : ls -a

f.Show all files in human readable formate

command : ls -lh

g.Sort modification time newest first

command : ls -lt

h. Shows the modification files oldest to newest

command : ls -ltr

i. Shows inode number

command : ls -i

j.Shows long list with inode number

command : ls -li

k.shows directory itself instead of listing it contents

command : ls -ld 

example : ls -ld /opt

l.List files and subdirectories

 command : ls -R

 It will enter directories and show their contents too.

m.Sort files size Decending order [largest first]

 command : ls -S

n.Sort file size Acending order[smallest first]

 command : ls -Sr

o.Sort files by extension

 command : ls -X or ls -lX

p. Add symbols by files and directories

 command : ls -F

 / -> directories

 * -> files

 @ -> link

2.Shows Present Working Directory 

 command : pwd

3.Change Directory

 commad : cd 

 example : cd /opt

4.Move to back directory 

 command : cd ..

5. Move to home(root) directory

  command : cd ~

6.Move to previous direcotory

 command : cd -

7.Stay in current direcotory

 command : cd .

Create Files and Directories
============================
8. Create empty file

 command : touch

 example : touch docker

 example : Create multiple files

 touch file1 file2 file3

 to create the files inside the directories

 syntax: touch /directory/file-name

 example: touch /opt/devops/file1.txt

 to create multiple files inside the directory use curly brackets {}

 touch /opt/devops/{file1,file2,file3}
 
9.Create a Directory

 command : mkdir

 example : mkdir project

 example : create multiple directories

 mkdir dir1 dir2 dir3

10.Create parent and child directories together

 command : mkdir -p

 example : mkdir -p devops/docker/project

==============================================
copy : cp is used to cpoy the files or directories from one location to another location

1. cp

syntax :

cp source destination

example : cp file1.txt /opt/project

2. Copy directories : cp -r is used to copy a directory along with all files and subdirectories inside it.
--> it can create destination directory automatically

command : cp -r

-r = recursivly -> it copies the complete directory sturucture and every thing inside it 

syntax : cp -r source_directory destination

example : cp -r /opt/devops /opt/devops-backup

3. cp -p : cp -p is used to copy a file while preserving its original attributes such as permissions, ownership where allowed, and timestamps.

syntax : cp -p source_file destination_file

example : cp -p /opt/file2.txt /root/file2-preserved.txt

4. cp -i : is used when you want Linux to ask for confirmation before overwriting an existing destination file.

syntax : cp -i source_file destination_directory/

example : cp -i file.txt file2.txt

5.cp -v : -v means verbose. It shows you what source file is being copied and where it is going.

example : 

[root@localhost opt]# cp -v freestyle /opt/linklab/
'freestyle' -> '/opt/linklab/freestyle'

we can use for copy subdirectory and file using -r

command : cp -rv

example : cp -rv /opt/devops /opt/devops-copy

